#!/bin/sh
# Refuses a commit made inside a Claude session when its author or its
# committer is the user's own address or the unconfigured-agent tripwire, or
# when the clone's own git config does not hold the tripwire identity; in the
# last case it first writes the tripwire back if another identity replaced it.
#
# Why: every worktree of a clone shares one .git/config, so any agent's
# `git config user.name` rewrites the author for every seat at once, and an
# agent's commit can go out under the user's own address on a public pull
# request. So each seat's identity lives in its launcher's environment
# (scripts/launch-claude-mac and scripts/launch-claude-ubuntu export
# GIT_AUTHOR_* and GIT_COMMITTER_* as <seat> and <seat>@nedschorus.invalid),
# and the shared config's identity is replaced with a tripwire,
# `unconfigured-agent <unconfigured-agent@nedschorus.invalid>`, rather than
# stripped: with no identity in the clone's config, git falls back to the
# Mac's ~/.gitconfig, which is the user's own name and junk@lerner1.com.
# This hook is the last safeguard: whatever the environment or the config holds, an
# agent's commit never goes out under the user's address, and a session
# started without the launcher is stopped at its first commit instead of
# publishing under the tripwire.
#
# The refused addresses are the user's own, each seen on commits in this
# repository's history: junk@lerner1.com (the Mac's ~/.gitconfig),
# ned@lerner1.com (commits by the reboot-test, merge-lane
# and nedlern identities), and nedlerner@yahoo.com (the merging account's
# GitHub identity). GitHub's own merge commits carry that last address and
# are right to: they are made on GitHub's servers and never pass through a
# local hook, so they are out of this hook's reach by construction, not by
# an exemption.
#
# Only commits made inside a Claude session are checked — one whose
# environment carries CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID or CLAUDE_CODE_BRIDGE_SESSION_ID,
# the same two variables scripts/git-client-side-hooks/prepare-commit-msg
# keys on. A commit the user types in his own terminal is his, under his own
# name, and is not this hook's business.
#
# Reach: git runs pre-commit for `git commit`, including --amend, and
# scripts/git-client-side-hooks/pre-merge-commit runs this same check for a
# merge commit made by `git merge`. `git rebase` and `git cherry-pick` run
# neither hook; they keep each commit's original author, and take the
# committer from the same environment the launcher sets. `git commit
# --no-verify` skips it, as it skips every pre-commit hook.
#
# Installed once per clone with prepare-commit-msg, by the same core.hooksPath
# (see that file's header); the reference checkout stays on main, so every
# seat's worktree runs main's copy.
#
# The tripwire is written once per clone by the user, never by an agent, since
# writing it stops every seat that is not launched with the exports above:
#   git -C <clone> config user.name unconfigured-agent
#   git -C <clone> config user.email unconfigured-agent@nedschorus.invalid
# Before the identity checks below, this hook reads the clone's own user.name
# and user.email with `git config --local`, which ignores ~/.gitconfig and the
# `-c` values of the command being run. When both are unset, as in a new
# clone, the hook refuses the commit, writes nothing, and prints those two
# commands for the user. When they hold anything but the tripwire, an agent
# has replaced it, so the hook writes the tripwire back and refuses the
# commit. The refusal is what makes
# the agent read the message, which says to tell the user what identity was
# found; a launched seat's own commit is otherwise unaffected, since its
# identity comes from the environment. A failed write is reported and refuses
# the commit.
#
# A commit the user types by hand in a clone is authored unconfigured-agent,
# since the clone's config outranks ~/.gitconfig, and outside a session this
# hook does not run to catch it. So the refusal's last line tells him to add
# `-c user.name=<name> -c user.email=<address>` after git. The -c form rather
# than --author because -c sets the committer too; --author alone would leave
# the commit committed by unconfigured-agent.
#
# The user's own commits inside a session are refused too. A commit the user
# types into a Claude session with the `!` prefix runs in the session's
# environment, carrying CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, so under his own address it is
# refused like an agent's, with no exemption, by the user's decision: he
# commits from a plain terminal. Nothing marks a command
# the user typed apart from one an agent ran, so an exemption would have
# nothing to key on.
#
# Amending. `git commit --amend` keeps the amended commit's author unless
# --reset-author is given, and git hands that author to this hook, so the
# same-command identity alone is refused again when the commit being amended
# carries the user's address; with --reset-author it is committed as the
# seat for both author and committer. Two cases need different instructions.
# A commit that went out under the user's address by mistake is the agent's
# own work and --reset-author puts the right name on it. A commit the user
# really wrote keeps his name, so the agent leaves it and commits on top.
#
# Why the refusal says to set the identity in the same command as the commit:
# the agent's Bash tool starts every call in a fresh shell, so an `export` in
# one call is gone by the next, and an agent that exports and then commits in
# two calls is refused again.
#
# The refusal printed below opens with what was refused and why, then gives
# one instruction per line, each with the condition it applies under, and
# cites no ruling and no date. The refused identity is named in the first two
# lines because the agent needs it to see which variable or flag put the
# user's address or the tripwire on this commit.
#
# Agent-homes. Before the identity checks, and for every commit, the user's own
# included, this hook refuses a commit in an agent-home: a worktree whose own
# git directory holds the file agent-home-marker, which
# scripts/launch-claude-mac, scripts/launch-claude-ubuntu and
# scripts/agent-home-seat-branch-commits-save-to-pushed-branch-and-detach-at-origin-main.py
# write. An agent-home rests on a detached HEAD at origin/main, so a commit
# there is on no branch, and nothing keeps it once HEAD moves. A marker rather
# than a path, because worktrees that are not agent-homes also sit under the
# agents root, and the Mac's agents root can be moved.

agent_home_git_directory="$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)" || {
    echo "pre-commit: git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir failed, so this hook could not tell whether this worktree is an agent-home, and refuses the commit; git's error is just above this line." >&2
    echo "Tell the user this whole message." >&2
    exit 1
}
if [ -e "$agent_home_git_directory/agent-home-marker" ]; then
    agent_home="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
    # The creator is named through this hook's own checkout, the reference checkout core.hooksPath points at, because an agent-home at an older commit may not have it.
    worktree_creator="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)/agent-seat-worktree-create-with-owner-file.py"
    echo "pre-commit: this commit is refused, because $agent_home is an agent-home: an agent-home holds no work and rests on a detached HEAD at origin/main, so a commit here is on no branch, and nothing keeps it once HEAD moves." >&2
    echo "Make the commit in a worktree of your own, on an nc-topic-branch, made with this command, which prints the new worktree's path: cd $agent_home && python3 $worktree_creator <branch name> --base HEAD" >&2
    echo "If the changes you meant to commit were made here in the agent-home, carry them to that worktree: git -C $agent_home diff --binary HEAD | git -C <the new worktree> apply --index; copy across any new file that git status lists as untracked, then, once the worktree holds them all, put the agent-home back with git -C $agent_home reset --hard HEAD and delete those copied files here." >&2
    echo "If you are the user committing by hand and mean this commit to be in the agent-home, add --no-verify to the same git commit command." >&2
    exit 1
fi

if [ -z "${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID:-}" ] && [ -z "${CLAUDE_CODE_BRIDGE_SESSION_ID:-}" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

TRIPWIRE_NAME="unconfigured-agent"
TRIPWIRE_EMAIL="unconfigured-agent@nedschorus.invalid"
clone_directory="$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)"
clone_config="$clone_directory/config"
# `git config --get` exits 1 for an unset key; any other nonzero exit is a failure to read.
config_read_failed() {
    echo "pre-commit: this commit is refused, because reading $1 from the clone's own git config, $clone_config, failed with exit $2; git's error is just above this line." >&2
    echo "Tell the user this whole message." >&2
    echo "Do not commit in this clone until the user says its git config reads again." >&2
    exit 1
}
clone_name="$(git config --local --get user.name)"
status=$?
[ "$status" -le 1 ] || config_read_failed user.name "$status"
clone_email="$(git config --local --get user.email)"
status=$?
[ "$status" -le 1 ] || config_read_failed user.email "$status"
if [ -z "$clone_name" ] && [ -z "$clone_email" ]; then
    echo "pre-commit: this commit is refused, because the clone's own git config, $clone_config, holds no user.name or user.email, so an agent-session started without its launcher would commit under whatever identity git finds elsewhere, such as the user's own ~/.gitconfig." >&2
    echo "Do not set the identity yourself: the first identity written in a clone is the user's to write." >&2
    echo "Tell the user that this clone holds no identity, and ask him to run these two commands:" >&2
    echo "git --git-dir=\"$clone_directory\" config user.name $TRIPWIRE_NAME" >&2
    echo "git --git-dir=\"$clone_directory\" config user.email $TRIPWIRE_EMAIL" >&2
    echo "Commit again only after the user says he has run them." >&2
    exit 1
fi
if [ "$clone_name" != "$TRIPWIRE_NAME" ] || [ "$clone_email" != "$TRIPWIRE_EMAIL" ]; then
    found="${clone_name:-(no user.name)} <${clone_email:-no user.email}>"
    if ! git config --local user.name "$TRIPWIRE_NAME" || ! git config --local user.email "$TRIPWIRE_EMAIL"; then
        echo "pre-commit: this commit is refused, because the clone's own git config, $clone_config, holds the identity $found instead of $TRIPWIRE_NAME <$TRIPWIRE_EMAIL>, and writing $TRIPWIRE_NAME back failed; git's error is just above this line." >&2
        echo "Tell the user that $clone_config holds $found, that this hook could not write $TRIPWIRE_NAME back, and what git's error says." >&2
        echo "Do not commit in this clone until the user has set the identity back." >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "pre-commit: this commit is refused, because the clone's own git config, $clone_config, held the identity $found instead of $TRIPWIRE_NAME <$TRIPWIRE_EMAIL>, which stops an agent-session started without its launcher from committing; this hook has written $TRIPWIRE_NAME back." >&2
    echo "Tell the user that $clone_config held $found, and that this hook set it back to $TRIPWIRE_NAME." >&2
    echo "Then commit again." >&2
    exit 1
fi

refused_identity=""
for role in AUTHOR COMMITTER; do
    if ! identity="$(git var "GIT_${role}_IDENT")"; then
        echo "pre-commit: git var GIT_${role}_IDENT failed, so this commit's $(printf '%s' "$role" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z') was not checked for the user's own address or the unconfigured-agent address, and this hook does not refuse the commit for it; git's error is just above this line." >&2
        echo "Before you push this commit, run git log -1 --format='%an <%ae> / %cn <%ce>' and check that neither identity is the user's address or unconfigured-agent." >&2
        echo "If git var keeps failing, tell the user this whole message." >&2
        continue
    fi
    # "Name <email> 1727000000 +0000" -> the email, lowercased.
    email="$(printf '%s\n' "$identity" | sed -n 's/.*<\([^>]*\)>.*/\1/p' | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')"
    case "$email" in
        (junk@lerner1.com|ned@lerner1.com|nedlerner@yahoo.com|unconfigured-agent@nedschorus.invalid)
            refused_identity="${identity% * *}"
            break ;;
    esac
done

[ -z "$refused_identity" ] && exit 0

echo "pre-commit: this commit is refused, because its author or committer is $refused_identity: an agent's commit must not carry the user's own address, which a pushed commit publishes, or the unconfigured-agent address, which marks an agent-session started without its launcher." >&2
echo "Commit under this seat's own name, not as $refused_identity." >&2
echo "If this session is a seat, relaunch it with launch-claude-mac or launch-claude-ubuntu, then commit again." >&2
echo "If you cannot relaunch, set the identity in the same command as the commit: GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=<seat> GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=<seat>@nedschorus.invalid GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=<seat> GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=<seat>@nedschorus.invalid git commit ..." >&2
echo "If you are amending a commit made under the user's address by mistake, add --reset-author to that same command." >&2
echo "If you are amending a commit the user wrote, leave it and make a new commit on top instead." >&2
echo "Do not change user.name or user.email in git config: every worktree of the clone shares that config, and a launched agent-seat's commits keep its own name either way, but an agent-session started without its launcher could then commit under the new identity." >&2
echo "If the user is committing by hand, commit from a terminal outside the Claude session, adding -c user.name=<name> -c user.email=<address> after git if the clone's identity is unconfigured-agent." >&2
exit 1
